  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 301 AM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nNorthwesterly flow aloft remains entrenched over the region\nbetween high amplitude ridging across the Rockies and western\nCONUS and troughing across the NE CONUS and into the Mid\nAtlantic. At the surface, the center of the high has drifted\noff to the east a bit, but winds remain light and out of the\neast to southeast. Conditions this early morning are quite\npleasant for late August, with temperatures in the mid to upper\n60s for most locations (upper 50s across NE Missouri). A few\nlight showers are ongoing across the region as of 3 AM, but\nthese are still dealing with dry low levels. The south\nsoutheasterly oriented low level jet is beginning to strengthen\na bit, but the strongest corridor of the LLJ will remain west of\nour CWA from roughly Salina into NE Oklahoma. CAMs suggest\nadditional convective development within a few hours, but this\nshould remain west and southwest of the CWA within the better\nLLJ dynamics (although perhaps some showers and storms could\nmake their way into Linn County KS). Meanwhile, a subtle \nshortwave and vort max translating through the northwesterly \nflow along the northeastern periphery of the upper ridge has \ninitiated an MCS across extreme SE South Dakota and into NE \nNebraska. This will continue to drift to the south southeast \nthis early morning, but should dissipate before reaching far NW \nMissouri. Dry conditions are likely for this afternoon, with\ndecent high level cloud cover lingering through early to mid\nafternoon. High temperatures this afternoon should range between\naround 80 degrees across NE Missouri to the mid 80s elsewhere\n(upper 80s south of the KC metro toward Linn County KS and Bates\nCounty MO). We will get some moisture return today, with dew\npoints increasing from their current values in the upper 50s to\nlower 60s to the mid 60s to perhaps even upper 60s. \n\nThe synoptic scale pattern remains mostly status quo heading \ninto tomorrow. The H5 high remains centered over New Mexico and \ninto west Texas, with mid/upper troughing across the NE CONUS \nand another upper trough moving across Saskatchewan into the \nUpper Midwest, flattening the ridge up north, as NW flow remains\nin place over the CWA. Temperatures should rebound into the mid\nto upper 80s Tuesday afternoon (lower 90s possible toward the \nButler area) with dew points increasing further by a couple \ndegrees. Weak height falls and strong daytime heating is likely \nto initiate scattered storm coverage across portions of south \nCentral Nebraska into northern Kansas by mid to late Tuesday \nafternoon, with this moving off to the east southeast through \nthe remainder of the afternoon hours and into Tuesday \nevening/night. The question is just how far east convection will\nbe able to maintain itself, but SPC has put far NE Kansas and NW\nMissouri into a marginal risk for severe storms for Tuesday\nevening, with chance PoPs (30 to 40%) across much of the CWA for\nTuesday evening/night. \n\nFor Wednesday through Friday, the H5 high is progged to migrate\nover the Desert Southwest, with high amplitude ridging across\nthe Rockies and as far north as the Canadian Plains of Alberta\nand Saskatchewan, with longwave upper troughing extending \nsouthward from Hudson Bay all the way through the Great Lakes \nand into the Ohio Valley. This will continue to place our CWA \nbeneath northwesterly mid/upper flow. Daily highs should be in \nthe mid to upper 80s (right at seasonal normals) with generally \ndry conditions expected. By this weekend, the upper ridge axis \nis progged to drift further east, so closer to our CWA, as an \nupper trough comes ashore across the Pacific Northwest. This \nshould lead to increased mid level heights and warmer 850 temps,\ntranslating to surface high temperatures into the upper 80s to \nlow to mid 90s and increased humidity for Saturday and Sunday.",
